   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n<div style=\"font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)\">\r\nRichard</div>\r\n<div style=\"font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)\">\r\n<br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div style=\"font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)\">\r\nI&#39;m not sure if this is a satisfactory answer to your question, but you could consult the &quot;Model Selection&quot; section of the example density surface model analysis found on our examples website:</div>\r\n<div style=\"font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12pt;color:rgb(0,0,0)\">\r\n<a href=\"http://examples.distancesampling.org/dsm-line-dolphins/mexico-analysis.html#model-selection\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://examples.distancesampling.org/dsm-line-dolphins/mexico-analysis.html%23model-selection&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792472467345000&amp;usg=AOvVaw13XGlu5SgqLRwx__A2hKNw\">http://examples.distancesampling.org/dsm-line-dolphins/mexico-analysis.html#model-selection</a><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<div style=\"width:100%;margin-top:16px;margin-bottom:16px;max-width:800px;min-width:424px\">\r\n<table role=\"presentation\" style=\"padding:12px 36px 12px 12px;width:100%;border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-color:rgb(200,200,200);border-radius:2px\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"border-spacing:0px\" valign=\"top\">\r\n<td style=\"width:100%\">\r\n<div style=\"font-size:21px;font-weight:300;margin-right:8px;font-family:&quot;wf_segoe-ui_light&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Light&quot;,&quot;Segoe WP Light&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,&quot;Segoe WP&quot;,Tahoma,Arial,sans-serif;margin-bottom:12px\">\r\n<a href=\"http://examples.distancesampling.org/dsm-line-dolphins/mexico-analysis.html#model-selection\" style=\"text-decoration:none;color:var(--themePrimary)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://examples.distancesampling.org/dsm-line-dolphins/mexico-analysis.html%23model-selection&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792472467345000&amp;usg=AOvVaw13XGlu5SgqLRwx__A2hKNw\">Example dsm analysis: pantropical dolphins in the Gulf of\r\n Mexico</a></div>\r\n<div style=\"font-size:14px;max-height:100px;color:rgb(102,102,102);font-family:&quot;wf_segoe-ui_normal&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,&quot;Segoe WP&quot;,Tahoma,Arial,sans-serif;margin-bottom:12px;margin-right:8px;overflow:hidden\">\r\nTypically (i.e. for other datasets) it will be necessary divide the transects into segments, and allocate observations to the correct segments using a GIS or other similar package 1, before starting an analysis using dsm.. Shapefiles and converting units</div>\r\n<div style=\"font-size:14px;font-weight:400;color:rgb(166,166,166);font-family:&quot;wf_segoe-ui_normal&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,&quot;Segoe WP&quot;,Tahoma,Arial,sans-serif\">\r\n<a href=\"http://examples.distancesampling.org\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://examples.distancesampling.org&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792472467346000&amp;usg=AOvVaw33G8WYCZXETkJK2VZT3uFs\">examples.distancesampling.org</a></div>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<br>\r\n<div></div>\r\n<hr style=\"display:inline-block;width:98%\">\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\"><font face=\"Calibri, sans-serif\" style=\"font-size:11pt\" color=\"#000000\"><b>From:</b> <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">distance...@googlegroups.com</a> &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">distance...@googlegroups.com</a>&gt; on behalf of Giliba Richard &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">nyan...@gmail.com</a>&gt;<br>\r\n<b>Sent:</b> 15 July 2022 13:54<br>\r\n<b>To:</b> <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">distance...@googlegroups.com</a> &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">distance...@googlegroups.com</a>&gt;<br>\r\n<b>Subject:</b> {Suspected Spam} [distance-sampling] Re: Importance of the explanatory variables in a density surface model</font>\r\n<div> </div>\r\n</div>\r\n<div></div></div>"
